WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:03.930
He certainly didn't come across

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:06.810
as anybody who was out to swindle you.

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:09.600
We just brainstorm and
then we execute ideas

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.400
and we fine tune them.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.050
He didn't come across as anybody

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:15.360
who had evil in his heart.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:16.980
You know, how can we be better in this?

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:18.630
How can we be better in that?

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:22.140
Or wanted to hurt anybody
or who you couldn't trust.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.240
We've been doing this for some time now

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:27.033
and we immediately
respond to those concerns.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.250
He didn't come across
as any of those things.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:33.870
It doesn't become less bizarre.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:35.760
When I tell you the details.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.130
He was running a nursing home company

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:39.510
and he all of a sudden becomes

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:42.480
one of the biggest mine
owners in Appalachia.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.780
I guess I would almost call
it a restoration economy.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.060
We're restoring the environment.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:50.700
He called himself an environmentalist.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:54.149
He was going to save the planet
and save the coal industry.

00:00:54.149 --> 00:00:58.350
He had a scheme to fund reclamation.

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:02.250
To us it seemed like circular
logic from the beginning.

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.650
Struggled to make his loan
payments to the state,

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:06.600
abandoned his plans, dashing hopes

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.130
he had raised in the town.

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:11.670
It was always just some great thing

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:12.930
that was about to happen.

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:14.343
The check was in the mail,

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:16.950
but it never was.

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:19.170
If somebody asked me my advice

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:20.790
about doing business with Tom Clarke,

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:22.443
I'd say, run and hide.

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:29.163
What's going on, man?

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:31.293
Why?

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.903
So what you going to do?

00:01:34.903 --> 00:01:37.560
I don't really believe
in missions for papers.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:40.350
Just try to be an honest,
old fashioned newspaper.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:42.207
Print what's interesting and true

00:01:42.207 --> 00:01:45.153
and informative and there we are.

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:48.660
I first heard about Tom Clarke,

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:52.440
first heard his name, in February 2014,

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:55.683
when he bought the Natural
Bridge of Virginia.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:00.360
It's what Rockbridge
County is named after.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.030
So Natural bridge is a big deal here.

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:05.130
And the bridge was up for sale,

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.530
and it's about 1,600 acres of land

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:09.210
and it was going to get chopped into

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:12.450
a pile of different
pieces and auctioned off.

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:16.293
And people around here were
horrified with that notion.

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:19.740
Tom Clarke, the community
viewed him as someone

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:22.590
who rode in here on a white
horse with a white hat

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:24.810
who would save Natural Bridge and could do

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:27.160
God knows what else to
help with the community.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:31.260
Then he fell behind almost instantly

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:33.540
in his payments to the state.

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:36.540
And one of the things
that he told the state,

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:39.630
and he told me, was he
was going to get into

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:44.630
the coal business to make the
payments for Natural Bridge.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.883
To the extent we can make
thermal coal cleaner.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:50.490
We've got an opportunity to actually

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:53.523
be able to sell more coal
out of West Virginia.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:57.180
When Tom Clarke came along,

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:59.850
the coal industry was in
a really deep downturn.

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:02.280
All the big coal companies went broke.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:06.090
They were out of cash, and
they had a lot of old mines

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:07.690
that hadn't been cleaned up yet.

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:10.890
Federal law requires that

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.740
when a coal mine operation is complete,

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:17.523
that they restore the land
back to its original condition,

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:21.720
restore it to its original
contours and plant grass on it,

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:23.610
and clean up all the poisoned water

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:25.233
caused by the mining activity.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:29.100
And so, the big coal companies
came up with this plan

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:34.100
to essentially shift those
obligations onto other people.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:37.893
And Tom Clarke was one
of those other people.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:41.730
He had no background in coal whatsoever.

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:42.563
He was in the Navy.

00:03:42.563 --> 00:03:45.240
He worked in finance and accounting jobs.

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.250
He built a nursing home company

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:48.780
that got really big at one point,

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:51.720
like 100 nursing homes, and
then sort of went broke.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:54.360
And he ended up taking
a few of those homes

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.430
and forming a company called Kissito.

00:03:56.430 --> 00:04:00.600
At Kissito Healthcare,
over 96% of our patients

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:02.100
would refer us to a friend.

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:04.590
And to me, that's...

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:06.360
Certainly the most unlikely person

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:11.133
to take over 100 Appalachian coal mines.

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:16.500
Tom Clarke, we had not
heard of him before.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:18.780
He did not own any coal companies

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:21.900
and hadn't really been involved in mining,

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:24.420
but he took on a bunch of permits

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:27.420
that were previously
owned by Patriot Coal.

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:30.330
Patriot Coal in the coal fields

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:33.210
is sometimes called a liability dump

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:34.710
Two of the biggest coal companies,

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:39.060
Peabody and Arch, spun off
kind of their unwanted mines

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:40.470
and a lot of their healthcare

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:43.770
and pension obligations
as separate companies.

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:45.420
And then those two companies merged

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:47.730
and that's what Patriot is.

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:51.180
And some people believe
that from the very beginning

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:53.850
these companies were set up to fail.

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:56.700
So Patriot went bankrupt in 2015

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:58.470
and that's where Tom Clarke came in.

00:04:58.470 --> 00:04:59.760
He swooped in and said,

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:02.700
I'll take all those mines
that nobody else wants.

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:03.866
He said he was going to clean up

00:05:03.866 --> 00:05:05.580
all these old derelict mines,

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:07.890
but he was also going to
develop this new market

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:11.460
for carbon neutral coal
where the carbon emissions

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:12.990
from the coal would be offset

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:14.913
by planting trees somewhere else.

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:18.960
When he said he was going
to save the coal industry

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:21.153
and save the planet all at once,

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:24.693
I burst out laughing,

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:27.350
because it doesn't make any sense.

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:30.960
Well, I'm going to get these mines,

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:33.840
I'm going to sell the coal
for a little bit more,

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:36.840
because I'm going to plant
trees all over the place.

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:39.690
And the consumers of coal are going to be

00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:41.580
happy to pay a little more knowing

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:43.740
that I'm going to plant trees.

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:46.800
Well, there wasn't any indication
that he was planting trees

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:48.250
or that he could plant trees.

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:52.320
And so, this notion of
somehow I'm going to be

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:56.613
a wonderful shade of
green coal mine operator,

00:05:57.660 --> 00:05:58.743
just gimme a break.

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:02.790
The idea of trees plus
mines has challenges.

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:06.330
It's unclear who would
buy this greener coal,

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:07.740
again, Tom Clarke.

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:09.270
I guess we're sort of betting the farm

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:11.730
that we're going to create a market.

00:06:11.730 --> 00:06:13.380
Environmentalists hated his idea.

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:17.130
They didn't want him to find
new ways to dig more coal.

00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:19.740
And so, really from the beginning,

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:22.380
there was no prospect of a market

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:25.320
for this kind of coal
package with carbon credits

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:26.320
that he was selling.

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:29.943
I don't know if he had a grand plan.

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:33.180
You know, part of me thinks

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:36.840
what he did with Natural
Bridge established his cred

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:38.973
in the environmental world.

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:41.460
I'm this great environmentalist.

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:44.040
I've saved Natural Bridge and I've done it

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:47.310
by setting up the Virginia
Conservation Legacy Fund

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:48.150
for God's sake.

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:49.590
Who could argue with a name like that?

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:51.570
Tom Clarke and Kim McCoy

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:54.760
of the Virginia Conservation Legacy Fund.

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:56.810
Let's give this team a round of applause.

00:06:58.383 --> 00:07:00.450
Part of me thinks that
he got Natural Bridge

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:03.048
in order to climb into the coal business.

00:07:03.048 --> 00:07:05.820
And part of me thinks he just

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:08.520
stumbled from one thing into the other.

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:10.320
Clarke had a lot of stuff.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:12.360
He had an iron mine in Minnesota.

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:14.340
He had a gold mine in California.

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:16.320
He also tried to corner the market

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:19.830
on offshore oil and gas platforms.

00:07:19.830 --> 00:07:22.530
He owned a historic Coast Guard vessel,

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:25.620
definitely more than a
billion dollars of assets.

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:27.180
He was totally a mogul,

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:28.980
but it was all other people's money.

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:35.280
Clarke did do a lot of
coal mine reclamation.

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:37.920
He spent tens of millions of dollars.

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:39.990
However, eventually he
just ran out of money.

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:41.610
He just didn't have anymore

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:44.223
and was unable to even
do basic maintenance.

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:47.190
Towards the end of his operation,

00:07:47.190 --> 00:07:49.260
he stopped paying the company

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:51.030
that took all the water samples

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:53.820
to test all the water around his mines.

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:55.350
When the state finally put

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:57.840
his company in receivership in 2020,

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:00.360
they said that they had
issued something like

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:02.883
160 notices of violations.

00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:15.720
It's a great question.

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:18.150
Did he somehow find a way of making money

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:20.010
off of all these deals?

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:23.820
They all ended up in
bankruptcy or receivership

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:25.110
of one kind or another.

00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:28.470
But is there some way he
could have shifted money

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:32.730
to himself somewhere where
that nobody knows about.

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:34.500
Mr. Clarke, you on the phone?

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:35.940
I am, Your Honor, good morning.

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:37.200
Good morning.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:40.200
For his part, he was
forced to go into court

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:42.510
and basically say, I'm totally broke.

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:43.413
It's all gone.

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:47.640
And if you look at the dozens of lawsuits

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:49.230
that creditors have brought against him,

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:52.560
they've accused him of improperly
moving money around a lot.

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:56.010
But oftentimes it's to take
money from one doomed project

00:08:56.010 --> 00:08:57.360
that's about to go bankrupt

00:08:57.360 --> 00:08:59.640
and put it into another doomed project

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:03.090
that's also eventually going
to fall out of his control.

00:09:03.090 --> 00:09:04.470
Anything else we need to take up

00:09:04.470 --> 00:09:06.030
in this AP at this morning?

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:07.410
No, your Honor.
All right, thank...

00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:08.464
Thank you, Your Honor.

00:09:08.464 --> 00:09:09.297
Thank you very much.

00:09:09.297 --> 00:09:10.130
Thank you all.

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:16.170
At the time Clarke was
doing all this stuff,

00:09:16.170 --> 00:09:18.270
he was actually very chatty.

00:09:18.270 --> 00:09:20.130
He was talking to the media all the time

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:22.620
about his grand plans.

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:24.750
Reforestation in West Virginia.

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:26.340
It puts people back to work.

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:30.330
It's part of a transition
economy, a restoration economy.

00:09:30.330 --> 00:09:33.630
You know, we'd talk on the
phone fairly regularly.

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:36.330
He'd say, my lawyers tell
me I shouldn't talk to you.

00:09:36.330 --> 00:09:37.880
And then he'd sing like a bird.

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:41.220
But since things went south

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:43.770
and virtually all of
his companies collapsed,

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:48.690
he's kind of not apparently in a position

00:09:48.690 --> 00:09:50.760
to talk to anybody right now about it.

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:52.950
Hi, it's message for Tom Clarke.

00:09:52.950 --> 00:09:55.410
This is Zach Mider calling
from Bloomberg News.

00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:59.400
I called, emailed and
sent a number of letters

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:01.740
to various addresses he uses,

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:02.943
but I never heard back.

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:10.380
This has been kind of

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:12.450
an uncomfortable topic in West Virginia,

00:10:12.450 --> 00:10:15.840
because when Clarke's company went broke

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:17.670
the state actually had to step in

00:10:17.670 --> 00:10:19.170
and place it in this kind of

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:21.810
unusual unprecedented receivership,

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:24.480
because they were afraid that the damage

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:27.060
that this company could
cause if it was just allowed

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:29.820
to go into an uncontrolled bankruptcy,

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:33.210
could kind of take down
the entire coal industry.

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:35.220
So they had to sort of get control of it

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:37.740
and essentially, have a
court-appointed receiver

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:40.773
control it to keep it from
causing collateral damage.

00:10:42.690 --> 00:10:44.880
Tom Clarke was an extreme example,

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:48.150
but he is not unique.

00:10:48.150 --> 00:10:53.070
We have seen a lot of mines
transferred to coal companies

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:55.890
that don't seem like they're really up

00:10:55.890 --> 00:10:58.893
for managing or reclaiming those mines.

00:10:59.730 --> 00:11:03.120
And in theory, regulators should be able

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:05.940
to stop these transfers from happening.

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:07.410
If they don't think it's a good idea

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:08.970
or it won't work out,

00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:11.970
they can try to stop the transfers.

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:13.890
And perhaps more importantly,

00:11:13.890 --> 00:11:17.070
the states want the coal
mines transferred as well,

00:11:17.070 --> 00:11:19.710
because they don't want
the reclamation liability

00:11:19.710 --> 00:11:22.980
to fall to the state agencies.

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:26.100
So there's a lot of
incentive for regulators

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:28.020
to approve mine transfers,

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:33.020
even if they're to really
weird guys like Tom Clarke.

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:39.510
So, in some ways, Tom Clarke
is kind of the fall guy.

00:11:39.510 --> 00:11:42.150
He's the one who the coal industry found

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:44.670
who was willing to kind of ride

00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:47.880
these old mines into the ground.

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:49.770
He was the guy willing
to hold the hand grenade

00:11:49.770 --> 00:11:51.273
when it was about to go off.

00:11:52.110 --> 00:11:56.361
Tom, you and your vision
for what this can be

00:11:56.361 --> 00:11:59.640
is truly a blessing to
Southern West Virginia.

00:11:59.640 --> 00:12:02.040
He obviously, was seen as a visionary,

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:03.590
because he was written up

00:12:03.590 --> 00:12:08.590
in all these high-powered
newspapers around the country.

00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:10.590
Coal miners lives would be better again

00:12:10.590 --> 00:12:12.930
and the environment would get straight

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:15.270
and he'd fix all these things.

00:12:15.270 --> 00:12:17.760
So yeah, anybody who says
they're going to do that,

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:19.710
if you believe it, they're a visionary.

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:25.260
Whether he was a visionary
or not, time will tell.

